WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.309
In Reactor, demand travels up the chain.

2
00:00:05.309 --> 00:00:09.418
The subscriber calls request(n), which means I can take n more.

3
00:00:09.418 --> 00:00:12.875
A source like Flux.range sends at most n, then waits.

4
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:14.398
A ticker can't wait.

5
00:00:14.398 --> 00:00:18.356
Flux.interval ticks once a millisecond, whatever you request.

6
00:00:18.356 --> 00:00:23.936
Behind it, a screen asks for one tick at a time, and takes 10 milliseconds to draw it.

7
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:27.901
Tick 1 comes before anyone asked for it, and the stream fails.

8
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:32.238
The onBackpressure operators let you choose what happens instead.

9
00:00:32.238 --> 00:00:39.816
Each one requests unbounded, so the source never overflows, and then decides what to do with the ticks nobody asked for.

10
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:44.356
onBackpressureBuffer with no size keeps every tick in a queue.

11
00:00:44.356 --> 00:00:47.215
The screen gets 0, 1, 2, 3, all in order.

12
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.469
But after 20 frames, the ticker is at 204.

13
00:00:50.469 --> 00:00:56.657
More than 180 ticks wait in memory, and the backlog grows until the heap runs out.

14
00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:59.476
Give the buffer a size, like 10.

15
00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:02.969
While the screen draws tick 0, ticks 1 to 10 fill it.

16
00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:04.770
Then a tick finds no room.

17
00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:07.389
The buffer cancels the source and fails.

18
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:10.125
The error waits behind the buffered ticks.

19
00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:13.923
The screen still gets 0 to 11, in order, and then the error.

20
00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:17.150
onBackpressureDrop keeps nothing.

21
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:21.063
Each tick that arrives while nobody is asking is thrown away.

22
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:26.815
So the screen sees ticks about 11 apart, and in 20 frames about 200 are dropped.

23
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:32.491
onBackpressureLatest keeps one slot, and each new tick replaces the one before.

24
00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:36.571
When the screen asks, it gets the newest: about every tenth tick.

25
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.765
onBackpressureError fails at once, at the first tick nobody asked for.

26
00:01:42.933 --> 00:01:45.206
None of these slows the source down.

27
00:01:45.206 --> 00:01:47.099
They only decide what to lose.

28
00:01:47.099 --> 00:01:50.697
Real backpressure needs a source that honours request(n).

29
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.256
So pick by the kind of feed.

30
00:01:53.256 --> 00:01:58.096
Prices, positions and statuses want latest, because newer replaces older.

31
00:01:58.096 --> 00:02:00.417
Samples and metrics can be dropped.

32
00:02:00.417 --> 00:02:07.179
Card authorizations and audit events must not be lost, so they get a bounded buffer that fails loudly.

33
00:02:07.179 --> 00:02:10.428
Error is for feeds where falling behind is a bug.

34
00:02:10.428 --> 00:02:13.477
And the unbounded buffer suits almost nothing.

35
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.969
In Kotlin Flow, a slow collector simply suspends the emitter.

36
00:02:17.969 --> 00:02:26.928
When a buffer sits between them, conflate keeps the newest, like onBackpressureLatest, and a full buffer suspends the emitter instead of failing.
